# 700-BLT-0003 — `bt_hci` listens only when the disk asks: its inquiries left the Ryzen's Wi-Fi deaf — done

**Type:** driver (`bt_hci`) · **Owner:** `BLT` · **Priority:** P1 · **Status:** done (2026-11-20) · **Blocked by:** — · **Main task:** [700](../issues/700-bluetooth.md), [801](../issues/801-the-bench-stays-manageable.md) · **Constitution:** MC-6.1, MC-12.1

## Problem

**On the Ryzen, 2026-11-19/20** (`fast-test` 92c2c6e9), the RTL8852BE's Wi-Fi stopped hearing anything for 10 s, and `rtw89_wifi` restarted itself (`RECEIVE STALLED … (801-DRV-0071)`). That happened 3 times in a 30-minute watch, once at boot, and during a release's download, whose TCP stream then broke. Each time the last frame was heard during `bt_hci`'s BR/EDR inquiry (10.24 s, once at start and every 5 minutes), on the same chip's Bluetooth:

| inquiry ends | last frame heard | stall logged |
|---|---|---|
| 22.2 | 21.5 | 31.5 |
| 342.5 | 340.8 | 350.8 |
| 662.8 | 659.9 | 669.9 |
| 2264.3 | 2260.3 | 2270.3 |

Not every inquiry did it: 4 of 8 left the Wi-Fi as it was. The Wi-Fi did not come back by itself after the inquiry ended either. The firmware's coexistence gives the radio to the Bluetooth for the inquiry, and our port sends only the station's first policy (213-DRV-0052), not Linux's TDMA for an inquiry. The rounds exist to show that a controller hears (700-BLT-0002). No user of them exists yet.

## Plan

1. `bt_hci` brings the controller up as before: reset, patch, address. It then listens only when `data/bt-listen.txt` is on the disk, and says which.
2. The coexistence for a Bluetooth inquiry or page, Linux's way, belongs to `rtw89_wifi` (213-DRV-0052). That stays open for when `bt_host` needs inquiries.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] On the Ryzen, without the file: `UP: NOT LISTENING`, no inquiry, and no `RECEIVE STALLED` in 30 minutes caused by Bluetooth.
- [x] With the file, the rounds as before (700-BLT-0002).

## Resolution

`bt_hci` (landed in `main` 98f8431b9, round 155) starts listening only when `data/bt-listen.txt` is on the boot volume, and its line says which. Both criteria were seen on the Ryzen, 2026-11-20.

**Without the file.** Release 202611200150 (tree 5e987df8a) ran 2 h 6 min:
- `1.967 bt_hci: [BT] UP: NOT LISTENING (NO data/bt-listen.txt): A SCAN TAKES THE RADIO A WI-FI MAY SHARE`;
- not one inquiry or LE scan;
- two `RECEIVE STALLED` (4087 s, 6962 s) with the Bluetooth idle, each rejoined in 2 s. These are the remaining stuck frames of the Wi-Fi itself, not Bluetooth's. Before this change the stalls followed the inquiries' ends (the table above).

Release 202611200415 (0075 on the same tree) said the same at each boot.

**With the file.** Release 202611200415, the file written from Linux and taken off again after the test:
```
1.869 bt_hci: [BT] UP: LISTENING EVERY 300 S (data/bt-listen.txt)
22.174 bt_hci: [BT] ROUND: 0 LE AND 0 BR/EDR DEVICES HEARD; 0 EVENTS IN THE LE SCAN, 1 IN THE INQUIRY; 0 EVENT PACKETS DROPPED
22.174 bt_hci: [BT] INQUIRY COMPLETE AFTER 10253 MS, STATUS 00
30.400 rtw89_wifi: [RTW89] RECEIVE STALLED ON CHANNEL 8: NO FRAME FOR 10001 MS (115 BEFORE; 4 SENT, 0 ACKNOWLEDGED SINCE THE LAST): THE DATA MODE STOPS (801-DRV-0071)
```
The round runs as in 700-BLT-0002. Its inquiry still deafens the Wi-Fi, which is why the file stays off on the bench until 213-DRV-0052 gives the coexistence Linux's way.

